llmResponse.content:
To add error handling to the `calculate` function, you can incorporate some checks to ensure that the inputs are numbers. If they're not, you can throw an error. Here's an updated version of the function with basic error handling:

```javascript
function calculate(a, b) {
  if (typeof a !== 'number' || typeof b !== 'number') {
    throw new Error('Both arguments must be numbers');
  }

  return a + b;
}
```

This version of `calculate` will throw an error if either `a` or `b` is not a number. This prevents unexpected results or bugs that might occur if invalid inputs are provided. When using this function, you can also wrap your calls in a `try-catch` block to handle the error gracefully:

```javascript
try {
  const result = calculate(5, '3');
  console.log('Result:', result);
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Error:', error.message);
}
```

In this example, the error will be caught, and you'll see a message in the console indicating that both arguments must be numbers.

